layout: layouts/base.njk
title: Search
---
{#- Pagefind's Component UI, which 1.5 recommends over the Default UI the page
    used to build with `new PagefindUI({…})`. It is custom elements rather than a
    constructor call, so the page says what it wants in markup: a config element
    carrying the bundle path, an input, a result count and the results.

    `bundle-path` goes through the `url` filter, unlike the href and src beside
    it: EleventyHtmlBasePlugin adds /Numina/ to those two itself and would add it
    twice, but it does not touch an attribute it has never heard of. The old
    constructor call wrote the path relative (`../pagefind/`), which a modal on
    pages at four different depths cannot do.

    Both files are vendored under pagefind/ by `npm run build`. Nothing here
    reaches offsite. -#}
<div class="shell shell--single">
  <main id="main" tabindex="-1">
    <h1>Search</h1>
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="/pagefind/pagefind-component-ui.css">
    <pagefind-config bundle-path="{{ '/pagefind/' | url }}" preload></pagefind-config>
    <div class="search-page">
      <pagefind-input placeholder="Search the rulebook and the lore…" autofocus></pagefind-input>
      <pagefind-summary default-message="Type to search the rulebook and the lore."></pagefind-summary>
      <pagefind-results max-sub-results="3"></pagefind-results>
    </div>
    <noscript><p>Search needs JavaScript. Try the <a href="/lore/">Lore</a> and <a href="/mechanics/">Mechanics</a> indexes instead.</p></noscript>
    <script src="/pagefind/pagefind-component-ui.js" defer></script>
    <script>
      // The ?q= handoff from the header form, which posts here with GET. The
      // Component UI has no constructor to call triggerSearch on, so the term
      // goes in the way a visitor would put it there: set the value, fire the
      // input event the component is already listening for. DOMContentLoaded is
      // late enough — a deferred script has run by then, and a custom element
      // already in the document renders during the define() call that upgrades
      // it, so pagefind-input's own <input> exists.
      window.addEventListener("DOMContentLoaded", function () {
        var q = new URLSearchParams(location.search).get("q");
        if (!q) return;
        var input = document.querySelector("pagefind-input input.pf-input");
        if (!input) return;
        input.value = q;
        input.dispatchEvent(new Event("input", { bubbles: true }));
      });
    </script>
  </main>
</div>
